<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hannibal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">247–c. 183 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Hannibal Barca · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcid-family">Barcid family</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hannibal Barca was a Carthaginian general who led the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. After crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> he won major victories over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, including at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>, but could not force Rome to surrender. He was defeated at Zama, later took part in Carthaginian government, and remains the most famous figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hannibal.jpg" alt="Hannibal" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fratelli Alinari · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hannibal_Barca_bust_from_Capua_photo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hannibal was one of the sons of the Carthaginian general and statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a> and an unknown mother, and was most likely born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. His name combines the name Hanno with the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal-hammon">Baal Hammon</a>, and its exact vocalisation is debated. He had two brothers, Hasdrubal and Mago. His brothers-in-law, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair">Hasdrubal the Fair</a> and the Numidian king Naravas, were close associates of his father in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary-war">Mercenary War</a> and the Punic conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. Rome's defeat of Carthage in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> had left deep tension, and Hannibal pledged to his father never to be a friend of Rome.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 218 BCE Hannibal attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a>, an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, which sparked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. He invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> by way of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> with North African war elephants, and about 10,000 Celtic tribesmen joined his army in the Po Valley. He won victories at Ticinus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>. At Cannae in 216 BCE he seized the supply depot on the Apulian plain; the Roman army there is estimated by some at 100,000 but more likely 50,000 to 80,000.</p><p>The war then settled into a stalemate. Following the strategy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fabius-maximus">Fabius Maximus</a>, called Cunctator, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> refused open battle and wore down Hannibal's army with smaller forces. Carthaginian defeats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> prevented his reinforcement, and he held most of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for about 15 years without a decisive victory.</p><p>In 203 BCE a Roman invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> led to his recall to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zama">Battle of Zama</a> in 202 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had the superior cavalry, after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> of Numidia changed sides, while the Carthaginians had more infantry and 80 elephants. Hannibal was defeated, ending the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the peace left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> with an indemnity of ten thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, Hannibal was elected suffete, or chief magistrate. He reorganised the state's finances to end corruption and recover embezzled funds so that the indemnity could be paid without raising taxes. The reforms were unpopular with members of the Carthaginian aristocracy and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and he went into voluntary exile.</p><p>He served as a military adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III the Great</a> at the Seleucid court in his war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. After Antiochus lost at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magnesia">Battle of Magnesia</a>, Hannibal fled again, stopping in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and reaching the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>. There he was betrayed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and died by suicide, poisoning himself.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hannibal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Philip V's treaty with <b>Hannibal</b> in 215 BCE drew Rome across the Adriatic in the First Macedonian War (214–205 BCE), and the Second and Third Macedonian Wars turned Rome into the arbiter of Greece.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The cities survived as Roman allies, supporting Scipio against <b>Hannibal</b> in 205 BCE; the Etruscans received Roman citizenship in 90 BCE, Sulla repressed Etruria in 82 BCE and Volterra capitulated in 79 BCE, and by 27 BCE the whole territory lay within the new Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Strabo and Plutarch say <b>Hannibal</b> was received at the court of Artaxias I and, in a story they also tell, planned and supervised the building of Artaxata, though the tale is reckoned apocryphal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Zama in 202 BCE Masinissa's cavalry helped decide Rome's victory over <b>Hannibal</b>, and the peace of 201 BCE confirmed him as king of both Massylian and Masaesylian lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It funded <b>Hannibal</b>’s Italian campaign and long sustained the war against Rome despite Rome’s far larger citizen population and Italian alliance network.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Against Philip V of Macedon it fought at Chios and Lade in 201 BCE, and in the war against Antiochus III its fleet under Eudamus defeated a Seleucid squadron commanded by <b>Hannibal</b> off Side in 190 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Died in Gebze, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.85,10.31&amp;year=-206" aria-label="Carthage on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4809 836 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hannibal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5259 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5259 1106h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.85,10.31&amp;year=-206">What was here in 206 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
