<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A certain peace is better and safer than a victory in prospect; the former is at your own disposal, the latter depends upon the gods.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to Scipio before Zama (Livy, book 30) &#183; tr. Cyrus R. Edmonds, 1850 &#183; Livy's account of his words to Scipio &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Rarely does that man consider the uncertainty of events whom fortune hath never deceived.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to Scipio before Zama (Livy, book 30) &#183; tr. Cyrus R. Edmonds, 1850 &#183; Livy's account of his words to Scipio &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;As far as relates to myself, time, (for I am returning to that country an old man which I left a boy,) and prosperity, and adversity, have so schooled me, that I am more inclined to follow reason than fortune.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to Scipio before Zama (Livy, book 30) &#183; tr. Cyrus R. Edmonds, 1850 &#183; Livy's account of his words to Scipio &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Alexander, king of Macedonia; because, with a small band, he defeated armies whose numbers were beyond reckoning; and because he had overrun the remotest regions, the merely visiting of which was a thing above human aspiration.&#8221;</p><footer>Livy, History of Rome, book 35 &#183; tr. Cyrus R. Edmonds, 1850 &#183; his answer at Ephesus when Scipio Africanus asked him who was the greatest general; Livy takes the story from the annalist Claudius, who had it from Acilius &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;To Pyrrhus; for he first taught the method of encamping; and besides, no one ever showed more exquisite judgment, in choosing his ground, and disposing his posts…&#8221;</p><footer>Livy, History of Rome, book 35 &#183; tr. Cyrus R. Edmonds, 1850 &#183; naming the second greatest general, in the same conversation with Scipio Africanus at Ephesus, as Livy reports it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
